Concurrency — No-Fight Parallel Rust Cashiers

Kategori: Rust  Minggu 12: Concurrency
Prerequisites: Week 11 — Smart Pointers.

Learning Objectives

new cashier transfers ownership belt shared safe (source: doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch16)
Rule: "fearless concurrency" — fights REJECTED by the compiler!

--Why This Matters (Non-IT)

2 cashiers decrementing stock together unlocked = wrong result (race!). In C/Go, mistakes surface at run (sometimes!). In Rust, the compiler forces  — wrong code doesn't compile. Sleep well.

--Program: 2 Safe Rust Cashiers

rust
use std::thread;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex, mpsc};

fn main() {
  // Shared safe (thread-count lock)
  let stock = Arc::new(Mutex::new(10));

  // Belt for 2 cashiers
  let (send, receive) = mpsc::channel();

  for cashier in 1..=2 {
    let s = Arc::clone(&stock);      // add owner
    let k = send.clone();            // add sender
    thread::spawn(move || {          // move: transfers ownership into thread!
      let mut stock = s.lock().unwrap(); // lock! (1 holder)
      *stock -= 1;
      k.send(format!("Cashier {} sold, {} left", cashier, *stock)).unwrap();
    }); // lock auto-released here
  }
  drop(send);

  for msg in receive {
    println!("{}", msg);
  }
  println!("Final stock: {} (exactly 8!)", stock.lock().unwrap());
}
--Key Concepts

 = New Cashier Bringing Lunch
 moves ownership into the thread (without it, dying borrows → rejected!).

 = Shared Safe
 shares ownership across threads,  locks (1 holder).  waits its turn.

 = Message Belt
 sends,  receives until senders run out.

--Beginner Friendly Explanation

Analogy: 2 Cashiers 1 Safe
Mutex = safe lock: 1 holds, others queue.
Arc = counted duplicate keys: all used up → safe dies safely.

